        "detokenised": "I will solve this using a recurrence relation.\nLet $a_n$ be the number of subsets of $\\{1, \\dots, n\\}$ with no consecutive integers and an even sum, and $b_n$ be the number of such subsets with an odd sum.\nLet $S_n$ be the set of valid subsets of $\\{1, \\dots, n\\}$.\nFor $n=1$:\nSubsets: $\\emptyset$ (sum 0, even), $\\{1\\}$ (sum 1, odd).\n$a_1 = 1, b_1 = 1$.\n\nFor $n=2$:\nSubsets: $\\emptyset$ (0, even), $\\{1\\}$ (1, odd), $\\{2\\}$ (2, even).\n$a_2 = 2, b_2 = 1$.\n\nFor $n=3$:\nSubsets: $\\emptyset$ (0, even), $\\{1\\}$ (1, odd), $\\{2\\}$ (2, even), $\\{3\\}$ (3, odd), $\\{1, 3\\}$ (4, even).\n$a_3 = 3, b_3 = 2$.\n\nGeneral recurrence:\nA valid subset of $\\{1, \\dots, n\\}$ either:\n1. Does not contain $n$: Then it is a valid subset of $\\{1, \\dots, n-1\\}$.\n2. Contains $n$: Then it cannot contain $n-1$, so it is $\\{n\\} \\cup$ a valid subset of $\\{1, \\dots, n-2\\}$.\n\nIf $n$ is even:\n- Subsets not containing $n$: $a_{n-1}$ even, $b_{n-1}$ odd.\n- Subsets containing $n$: Since $n$ is even, adding $n$ to a subset preserves parity. So $a_{n-2}$ even, $b_{n-2}$ odd.\n$a_n = a_{n-1} + a_{n-2}$\n$b_n = b_{n-1} + b_{n-2}$\n\nIf $n$ is odd:\n- Subsets not containing $n$: $a_{n-1}$ even, $b_{n-1}$ odd.\n- Subsets containing $n$: Since $n$ is odd, adding $n$ flips parity. So $b_{n-2}$ even, $a_{n-2}$ odd.\n$a_n = a_{n-1} + b_{n-2}$\n$b_n = b_{n-1} + a_{n-2}$\n\nLet's compute:\n$n=1: a_1=1, b_1=1$\n$n=2$ (even): $a_2 = a_1 + a_0 = 1 + 1 = 2$ (since $a_0=1, b_0=0$ for $\\emptyset$), $b_2 = b_1 + b_0 = 1 + 0 = 1$.\n$n=3$ (odd): $a_3 = a_2 + b_1 = 2 + 1 = 3$, $b_3 = b_2 + a_1 = 1 + 1 = 2$.\n$n=4$ (even): $a_4 = a_3 + a_2 = 3 + 2 = 5$, $b_4 = b_3 + b_2 = 2 + 1 = 3$.\n$n=5$ (odd): $a_5 = a_4 + b_3 = 5 + 2 = 7$, $b_5 = b_4 + a_3 = 3 + 3 = 6$.\n$n=6$ (even): $a_6 = a_5 + a_4 = 7 + 5 = 12$, $b_6 = b_5 + b_4 = 6 + 3 = 9$.\n$n=7$ (odd): $a_7 = a_6 + b_5 = 12 + 6 = 18$, $b_7 = b_6 + a_5 = 9 + 7 = 16$.\n$n=8$ (even): $a_8 = a_7 + a_6 = 18 + 12 = 30$, $b_8 = b_7 + b_6 = 16 + 9 = 25$.\n$n=9$ (odd): $a_9 = a_8 + b_7 = 30 + 16 = 46$, $b_9 = b_8 + a_7 = 25 + 18 = 43$.\n$n=10$ (even): $a_{10} = a_9 + a_8 = 46 + 30 = 76$, $b_{10} = b_9 + b_8 = 43 + 25 = 68$.\n\nCheck: Total subsets $F_{12} = 144$. $76 + 68 = 144$. Correct.\n\nANSWER: 76<turn|>"
